WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.348
Lesson 5 is where particle physics becomes more selective.

2
00:00:04.348 --> 00:00:08.696
Up to this point, a student can often describe particles, pairs, and interactions in words.

3
00:00:08.696 --> 00:00:13.044
But event questions now demand a harder filter.

4
00:00:13.044 --> 00:00:17.392
A proposed decay or reaction is not accepted because it sounds familiar.

5
00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:21.739
It is accepted only if the conserved quantities balance.

6
00:00:21.739 --> 00:00:26.087
Charge must balance.

7
00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:30.435
Baryon number must balance.

8
00:00:30.435 --> 00:00:34.783
Lepton number must balance.

9
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:39.131
These are not three optional checks.

10
00:00:39.131 --> 00:00:43.479
They are a combined screening system.

11
00:00:43.479 --> 00:00:47.827
One broken total is enough to reject the event.

12
00:00:47.827 --> 00:00:52.175
That makes this lesson richer than a simple formula lesson.

13
00:00:52.175 --> 00:00:56.523
It teaches students how to distrust a tempting story until the bookkeeping agrees.

14
00:00:56.523 --> 00:01:00.871
The worked example is much easier when that audit mindset is already in place.

15
00:01:00.871 --> 00:01:05.218
Keep this takeaway in view: A particle event is only acceptable when every conservation gate agrees at the same time.

16
00:01:05.218 --> 00:01:09.566
Gate one means charge.

17
00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:13.914
Total charge before and after must match exactly.

18
00:01:13.914 --> 00:01:18.262
Gate two means baryon number.

19
00:01:18.262 --> 00:01:22.610
Matter-balance stories must preserve baryon count.

20
00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:30.678
This conservation diagram is useful because it forces the learner to slow down in the right way.

21
00:01:30.678 --> 00:01:38.746
Instead of jumping straight from the particle names to a conclusion, the student has to build a before column and an after column, then attach the correct labels to each participant.

22
00:01:38.746 --> 00:01:46.813
Charge is usually the first check students remember, but the diagram makes it obvious that charge is only one part of the audit.

23
00:01:46.813 --> 00:01:54.881
Baryon number protects the matter story, and lepton number becomes especially important whenever beta decay, electrons, positrons, neutrinos, or antineutrinos are involved.

24
00:01:54.881 --> 00:02:02.949
The power of the board is not just that it shows three rules.

25
00:02:02.949 --> 00:02:11.017
It shows how the three rules work together on one event.

26
00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:19.084
Keep this takeaway in view: A visible ledger makes conservation feel like a rigorous audit rather than a vague memory rule.

27
00:02:19.084 --> 00:02:27.152
Before-and-after frame: Always write the full event before doing any conservation arithmetic.

28
00:02:27.152 --> 00:02:35.220
Triple audit: Charge, baryon number, and lepton number must all be checked, not just the easiest one.

29
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.344
Imagine a vault protected by three separate locks.

30
00:02:39.344 --> 00:02:43.468
One key opens the charge lock.

31
00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:47.592
Another opens the baryon-number lock.

32
00:02:47.592 --> 00:02:51.716
A third opens the lepton-number lock.

33
00:02:51.716 --> 00:02:55.841
Turning only one or two locks is not enough.

34
00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.965
The vault still stays closed.

35
00:02:59.965 --> 00:03:04.089
Conservation questions behave in the same way.

36
00:03:04.089 --> 00:03:08.213
Students sometimes feel encouraged when charge balances and then stop too early.

37
00:03:08.213 --> 00:03:12.337
This analogy breaks that habit.

38
00:03:12.337 --> 00:03:16.461
A reaction is not partly allowed.

39
00:03:16.461 --> 00:03:20.585
It is either allowed by all the locks together or blocked by the first lock that fails.

40
00:03:20.585 --> 00:03:24.709
The analogy matters because it changes the student's attitude from hopeful guessing to disciplined checking.

41
00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:28.834
Keep this takeaway in view: Conservation is a multi-lock decision: every lock has to open before the event is accepted.

42
00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:32.958
First lock means charge.

43
00:03:32.958 --> 00:03:37.082
Useful and quick, but never the only condition.

44
00:03:37.082 --> 00:03:41.206
Second lock means baryon number.

45
00:03:41.206 --> 00:03:45.330
This prevents impossible matter-accounting stories from slipping through.

46
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:50.430
The formal core of this lesson is simple to state and important to apply carefully.

47
00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:55.530
The total charge before must equal the total charge after.

48
00:03:55.530 --> 00:04:00.630
The total baryon number before must equal the total baryon number after.

49
00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.730
The total lepton number before must equal the total lepton number after.

50
00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:10.830
On paper, these three statements look compact.

51
00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:15.930
In real questions, they become a workflow.

52
00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.030
Students list the particles, assign the correct numbers, total the columns, and compare.

53
00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:26.130
What makes the lesson richer is that different reactions fail for different reasons.

54
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.230
Some fail immediately on charge.

55
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:36.330
Others pass charge but fail on baryon number.

56
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:41.430
Others survive both of those and fail only when lepton number is checked.

57
00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.530
That is why the formulas must be treated as a package.

58
00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:51.630
Keep this takeaway in view: The three conservation equations are strongest when they are used as one coordinated audit.

59
00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.730
Fast first pass: Charge is often the quickest screen, but it is only the first gate.

60
00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:01.830
Matter audit: Baryon number keeps track of baryons through decays and collisions.

61
00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:06.930
sum(charge)_before = sum(charge)_after.

62
00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:12.030
sum(baryon number)_before = sum(baryon number)_after.

63
00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:17.130
A reaction is accepted only when all three ledgers balance together.

64
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:22.482
A strong habit for this lesson is a four-step routine.

65
00:05:22.482 --> 00:05:27.834
First, freeze the event by writing the complete before-and-after particle list.

66
00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:33.186
Second, label every relevant particle with charge, baryon number, and lepton number.

67
00:05:33.186 --> 00:05:38.539
Third, total each side carefully.

68
00:05:38.539 --> 00:05:43.891
Fourth, decide immediately whether the channel survives or fails.

69
00:05:43.891 --> 00:05:49.243
This routine matters because it protects the learner from being seduced by familiar words like neutron, proton, electron, or neutrino.

70
00:05:49.243 --> 00:05:54.595
The particle names are not enough.

71
00:05:54.595 --> 00:05:59.947
The labels have to be carried all the way into the totals.

72
00:05:59.947 --> 00:06:05.299
Once students adopt this fixed pattern, event screening becomes calmer, faster, and more accurate.

73
00:06:05.299 --> 00:06:10.651
Keep this takeaway in view: Conservation questions become much more manageable when every event is processed through the same fixed audit routine.

74
00:06:10.651 --> 00:06:16.004
Step 1 means freeze the event.

75
00:06:16.004 --> 00:06:21.356
Write the full reaction before trying to judge it.

76
00:06:21.356 --> 00:06:26.708
Step 2 means label every particle.

77
00:06:26.708 --> 00:06:32.060
Attach charge, baryon number, and lepton number to every relevant participant.

78
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:38.911
This is where particle reactions start to feel genuinely advanced.

79
00:06:38.911 --> 00:06:45.762
A channel can look neat at first glance because the charge total balances perfectly, yet the event still fails because baryon number or lepton number does not survive the change.

80
00:06:45.762 --> 00:06:52.613
That is why one balanced column is never enough.

81
00:06:52.613 --> 00:06:59.464
The learner has to hold three ledgers in view at the same time and reject the reaction the instant one of them breaks.

82
00:06:59.464 --> 00:07:06.315
This extra slide matters because it trains the student to stop trusting familiar particle names or elegant-looking products too early.

83
00:07:06.315 --> 00:07:13.165
The event only becomes physically acceptable when every bookkeeping gate stays open together.

84
00:07:13.165 --> 00:07:20.016
Keep this takeaway in view: A believable-looking reaction is not an allowed reaction until all three conservation ledgers agree.

85
00:07:20.016 --> 00:07:26.867
Charge pass means still not enough.

86
00:07:26.867 --> 00:07:33.718
A reaction can keep charge balanced and still fail the full screening.

87
00:07:33.718 --> 00:07:40.569
Hidden failure means baryon or lepton number.

88
00:07:40.569 --> 00:07:47.420
The impossible part is often exposed only after the second or third ledger is checked.

89
00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:53.871
A very useful reaction family for this lesson is the beta-style event.

90
00:07:53.871 --> 00:08:00.322
The charge story may seem manageable, but the real discipline arrives when leptons enter the event list.

91
00:08:00.322 --> 00:08:06.772
If an electron appears, the learner should immediately ask what happened to the lepton ledger as a whole.

92
00:08:06.772 --> 00:08:13.223
Was a matching neutrino or antineutrino needed to keep the total consistent?

93
00:08:13.223 --> 00:08:19.674
Was a shortcut answer written that sounds familiar but leaves the bookkeeping incomplete?

94
00:08:19.674 --> 00:08:26.125
This richer step matters because many particle questions are not lost on the first formula.

95
00:08:26.125 --> 00:08:32.575
They are lost when one missing particle is ignored.

96
00:08:32.575 --> 00:08:39.026
Writing every participant clearly is what turns conservation from a slogan into a reliable decision tool.

97
00:08:39.026 --> 00:08:45.477
Keep this takeaway in view: Whenever leptons appear, the safest move is to write the full event and audit lepton number explicitly.

98
00:08:45.477 --> 00:08:51.928
Allowed pattern means all participants written.

99
00:08:51.928 --> 00:08:58.378
A channel only survives when every relevant particle needed for the conservation ledger is included.

100
00:08:58.378 --> 00:09:04.829
Rejected shortcut means missing lepton balance.

101
00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:11.280
A familiar reaction story can still fail if one lepton-side participant has been left out.

102
00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.346
Run four quick reminders.

103
00:09:15.346 --> 00:09:19.412
If charge balances but lepton number fails, is the event allowed?

104
00:09:19.412 --> 00:09:23.478
No.

105
00:09:23.478 --> 00:09:27.544
If baryon number changes across the event, can a familiar-looking reaction still be rejected?

106
00:09:27.544 --> 00:09:31.610
Yes.

107
00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:35.676
Should charge be checked alone or with the other ledgers?

108
00:09:35.676 --> 00:09:39.742
With the other ledgers.

109
00:09:39.742 --> 00:09:43.808
And what is the safest moment to reject a reaction?

110
00:09:43.808 --> 00:09:47.874
The instant one conservation rule fails.

111
00:09:47.874 --> 00:09:51.940
These checks matter because they turn conservation from a last-minute afterthought into the first serious filter.

112
00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:56.006
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the event can now be screened with a disciplined triple-ledger audit.

113
00:09:56.006 --> 00:10:00.072
Charge only means not enough.

114
00:10:00.072 --> 00:10:04.138
A balanced charge total does not rescue a broken baryon or lepton ledger.

115
00:10:04.138 --> 00:10:08.204
Baryon alert means matter bookkeeping.

116
00:10:08.204 --> 00:10:12.270
If baryon number changes improperly, the channel is rejected.

117
00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:17.577
You are ready for the worked example now.

118
00:10:17.577 --> 00:10:22.884
Start by writing the full reaction clearly.

119
00:10:22.884 --> 00:10:28.191
Then assign charge, baryon number, and lepton number to each relevant particle on both sides.

120
00:10:28.191 --> 00:10:33.499
Total the three ledgers carefully and compare before and after.

121
00:10:33.499 --> 00:10:38.806
If one ledger fails, reject the event immediately and say why.

122
00:10:38.806 --> 00:10:44.113
If all three survive, then the event has passed the main conservation screen.

123
00:10:44.113 --> 00:10:49.420
That is the discipline this lesson is trying to install: do not trust the reaction because it sounds familiar.

124
00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:54.727
Trust it only after the bookkeeping agrees.

125
00:10:54.727 --> 00:11:00.034
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the conservation lesson now has a full audit routine, not just a list of formulas.

126
00:11:00.034 --> 00:11:05.341
Step 1: Build the complete before-and-after event so nothing is hidden.

127
00:11:05.341 --> 00:11:10.649
Step 2: Attach every conservation label before you total anything.

128
00:11:10.649 --> 00:11:15.956
write the full before-and-after event.

129
00:11:15.956 --> 00:11:21.263
assign charge, baryon number, and lepton number to each particle.

130
00:11:21.263 --> 00:11:26.570
The safest reaction answers are bookkeeping-first, not intuition-first.
